      "writtenOn": "2025-01-14T08:07:44Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "\u003e This could definitely affect our parsing logic and I\u0027m quite concerned about this change as sched_blocked_reason is a load bearing event that a lot of people care about.\n\u003e \n\u003e Could you please provide a more detailed example with the fields of each event? It\u0027s not clear to me by just the event names whether the thread the sched_blocked_reason event is being emitted for is switching in or out.\n\nYes, here is a trace log showing the state transitions of dd-636 from Running to Uninterruptible, then to Runnable, and finally back to Running.\n\n```\n1. \u003cidle\u003e-0  [003] .... 725.002694: sched_switch: prev_comm\u003dswapper prev_pid\u003d0 prev_prio\u003d120 prev_state\u003dR \u003d\u003d\u003e next_comm\u003ddd next_pid\u003d636 next_prio\u003d120\n2.   dd-636  [003] .... 725.002790: sched_switch: prev_comm\u003ddd prev_pid\u003d636 prev_prio\u003d120 prev_state\u003dD \u003d\u003d\u003e next_comm\u003dswapper/3 next_pid\u003d0 next_prio\u003d120\n3.   dd-636  [003] .... 725.002792: sched_blocked_reason: pid\u003d636 io_wait\u003d1 caller\u003d__iomap_dio_rw\n4. \u003cidle\u003e-0  [003] .... 725.003506: sched_waking: comm\u003ddd pid\u003d636 prio\u003d120 target_cpu\u003d3\n5. \u003cidle\u003e-0  [003] .... 725.003510: sched_wakeup: comm\u003ddd pid\u003d636 prio\u003d120 target_cpu\u003d003\n6. \u003cidle\u003e-0  [003] .... 725.003520: sched_switch: prev_comm\u003dswapper prev_pid\u003d0 prev_prio\u003d120 prev_state\u003dR \u003d\u003d\u003e next_comm\u003ddd next_pid\u003d636 next_prio\u003d120\n```\n\nAnd here is the explanation:\n\n```\n1. The swapper is preempted by dd-636, triggering a process context switch, and dd-636 starts running on CPU3\n2. dd-636 enters an uninterruptible state, triggering a context switch, and swapper/3 starts running on CPU3\n3. sched_blocked_reason explains that dd-636 entered the uninterruptible state due to invoking __iomap_dio_rw and waiting for an I/O operation to complete.\n4. dd-636 is being woken up by try_to_wake_up but has not entered any CPU’s runqueue. Perfetto assumes dd-636 has entered the runnable state.\n5. dd-636 is woken up and placed in the runqueue of CPU3.\n6. The swapper is preempted by dd-636 again, triggering a process context switch, and dd-636 resumes running on CPU3. And Perfetto assumes dd-636 has entered the running state.\n```\n\n\u003e \n\u003e https://cs.android.com/android/platform/superproject/+/android14-qpr3-release:external/perfetto/src/trace_processor/importers/common/thread_state_tracker.cc contains the logic for us parsing schedblockreason.\n\nIt seems that it would be fine if the sched_blocked_reason occurs after the sched_switch.",
